  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "What surface is defined by the equation $r=3$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?",
  "worked_solution": "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nr=3\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $z$-axis equals $3,$ while $\\theta $ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the circular cylinder shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14045161/assets/aa435c0ad4b4482c85a05af32afbbb7819b330a4292762ec66bad30a6ed31a44.png)",
